Mythbeast Survivors

An offline, single-player, portrait 2D survivor game built with Godot and
GDScript. This foundation explores Whispering Grove, a woodland arena
featuring an original chibi adventurer, soft forest scenery, and local audio.

Getting started

  Install Godot 4.7.2 Standard.
  In the Godot Project Manager, import project.godot from this directory.
  Press F5 and select Play to enter Whispering Grove.

  The main menu also opens Settings and Credits. Settings controls the
  music and sound effects for the current session. Move with WASD or the
  arrow keys on desktop. On a touchscreen, press and drag below the HUD.
  Release to stop. Use Pause or Esc to open the pause menu.

Current scope

  Forest Slimes spawn in Whispering Grove and chase the hero. The hero casts
  Arcane Bolt automatically toward the nearest enemy in range. Enemy contact
  reduces health, with a brief recovery window between hits. Collect XP gems
  dropped by slimes. Leveling up pauses the encounter so you can choose an
  ability upgrade. At zero health, choose Try Again for a fresh encounter or
  Main Menu to leave. Each run starts with only Arcane Bolt. The run timer is
  a preview value.
  Android and iOS export configuration and device testing are also ahead.

Project layout

  core/app          Application entry, scene navigation, and audio lifetime
  features          Whispering Grove, combat, progression, and ability effects
  ui                Menus, HUD, safe-area layout, and shared theme
  data              Character, enemy, weapon, and ability definitions
  assets            Project artwork, application icon, and audio
  tests/smoke       Foundation navigation and behavior checks

Development checks

  Run these commands from this directory. Set GODOT_BIN if the executable
  is installed outside PATH.

    bash scripts/test-godot.sh --gate
    bash scripts/test-godot.sh

  The required gate runs a curated set of startup, navigation, movement,
  combat, and progression checks. The second runs the full local smoke suite.
  Add critical regressions to the gate when related behavior changes.

